summary:
**Figure 12.7** draws every one of the 36 boxes predicted for the giraffe image (`cutoff=None`, p.369). The grid is crowded with gray boxes labeled **"unlabeled 0.00"** or **"unlabeled 0.01"** (cells whose argmax label is 0, the no-object class) and a cluster of blue **"giraffe"** boxes around the animal with confidences such as 0.00, 0.02, 0.05, 0.07 and the one at 0.16 that survived the cutoff in figure 12.6.

The authors' diagnosis: the model **learns very low confidence values because it has not yet learned to consistently locate objects**. Since the confidence target is the IoU of the predicted box (p.367), poor localization means low targets.

To improve it, they suggest:

- **train for more epochs** (validation loss was still falling, p.368);
- **use the whole COCO dataset** (only images with at most four boxes were kept, p.360);
- **data augmentation**, e.g. translating and rotating input images *and* their boxes;
- **improve the class probability map for overlapping boxes** (Listing 12.7 ignored overlaps, p.364);
- **predict multiple boxes per grid location using a bigger output grid** (the original paper predicted several boxes per cell, p.360).

All of these would bring the setup closer to the original YOLO recipe, but the example is only meant to give a feel for detection training: an accurate COCO detector from scratch would need a large amount of compute and time. So the chapter moves to **section 12.3, Using a pretrained RetinaNet detector**. RetinaNet is **also a single-stage detector** working on the same basic principles as YOLO; the biggest conceptual difference, explained on p.371, is how it uses its ConvNet to handle small and large objects at once.
